**Steps to Reproduce**:

1. Install GitHub Copilot extension
2. Open Copilot Chat
3. Use "Delegate Session" with target set to "Local"
4. Click the model picker dropdown in the chat input
5. See error: `TypeError: i.charAt is not a function` in console

**Expected behavior**:

- Model picker should show available models without crashing, gracefully handling non-string metadata values from any provider.

**Root Cause**:

`getPriceCategoryLabel` in [modelPickerUtils.ts#L17](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/f38816eb094a5ad5372dd54ffcd38a9c7c235330/src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/browser/widget/input/modelPicker/modelPickerUtils.ts#L17) calls `priceCategory.charAt(0)` in the `default` switch case without a type guard.

When an extension provides a non-string `priceCategory` value (e.g. GitHub Copilot with delegate session "Local"), `.charAt()` doesn't exist and throws.

```ts
export function getPriceCategoryLabel(priceCategory: string | undefined): string | undefined {
    switch (priceCategory) {
        case undefined:
        case '':
            return undefined;
        case 'low':
            return localize('chat.priceCategory.low', "Low cost");
        case 'medium':
            return localize('chat.priceCategory.medium', "Medium cost");
        case 'high':
            return localize('chat.priceCategory.high', "High cost");
        case 'very_high':
            return localize('chat.priceCategory.veryHigh', "Very high cost");
        default:
            // ⚠️ BUG: priceCategory.charAt(0) crashes when priceCategory is not a string
            return localize('chat.priceCategory.unknown', "{0} cost", priceCategory.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + priceCategory.slice(1));
    }
}
```

**Suggested fix**:

Add a `typeof` guard before calling `.charAt()` in the default case:

```ts
export function getPriceCategoryLabel(priceCategory: string | undefined): string | undefined {
    switch (priceCategory) {
        case undefined:
        case '':
            return undefined;
        case 'low':
            return localize('chat.priceCategory.low', "Low cost");
        case 'medium':
            return localize('chat.priceCategory.medium', "Medium cost");
        case 'high':
            return localize('chat.priceCategory.high', "High cost");
        case 'very_high':
            return localize('chat.priceCategory.veryHigh', "Very high cost");
        default:
            return typeof priceCategory === 'string'
                ? localize('chat.priceCategory.unknown', "{0} cost", priceCategory.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + priceCategory.slice(1))
                : undefined;
    }
}
```

**Note**:

the sibling function `getCategoryLabel` in [modelPickerHover.ts#L215](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/f38816eb094a5ad5372dd54ffcd38a9c7c235330/src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/browser/widget/input/modelPicker/modelPickerHover.ts#L215) had the same bug and was already fixed on `main` with the same `typeof` guard pattern.

# Feature Request: Standardized Chat Panel Extension API (transcript access, scroll state, overlay decorations)

## Summary

Propose a cross-editor-standardized extension API that lets third-party extensions observe and decorate AI chat panels — the same way the existing `TextEditor` decoration API lets extensions decorate file editors (overview ruler, gutter icons, minimap markers).

This is the API prerequisite that would have let a plugin deliver the companion feature request **(companion UI feature request — to be filed separately and linked back here)** without waiting for first-party implementation. We're asking VS Code to lead the standardization so that Cursor, Windsurf, and other VS Code-family editors can adopt the same surface, giving plugin developers one target instead of N.

## Motivation

AI chat panels are now a core editing surface, but they are completely opaque to extensions:

- **No transcript access.** An extension cannot read the turns of the host's built-in chat (Copilot Chat, Cascade, Cursor Chat, etc.). Anything that wants to analyze, summarize, export, or visualize the conversation has to scrape the DOM or be its own chat — neither of which is a real solution.
- **No scroll state.** There is no `chat.scrollTop`, `scrollHeight`, or `visibleRanges` equivalent. Even for notebooks this is missing (microsoft/vscode#311289); for chat it was never proposed. Any "minimap for chat", "jump to turn N", "synced side panel", or "reading-position marker" feature is impossible without it.
- **No overlay decorations.** The `createTextEditorDecorationType` API — including `overviewRulerColor`, `overviewRulerLane`, `gutterIconPath`, `before`/`after` — only applies to `TextEditor`. There is no equivalent for chat panels, so the overview-ruler/heatmap/error-marker UX from the companion request cannot be built by a third party.

This gap forces every "nice chat UX" feature to be either first-party-only or reimplemented per-editor. A standardized API would let one plugin target VS Code, Cursor, Windsurf, VSCodium, etc. simultaneously — exactly the ecosystem benefit the existing extension API already provides for file editors.

## Proposed API surface

Illustrative, not prescriptive — the goal is to start the conversation, not to dictate the final shape.

### 1. Chat panel handle

```ts
namespace vscode.chat {
  // Handle to an open chat panel (sidebar or editor-embedded).
  export interface ChatPanel {
    readonly id: string;
    readonly viewType: string;          // e.g. "github.copilot-chat", "codeium.cascade", "cursor.chat"
    readonly visible: boolean;
    readonly onDidDispose: Event<void>;
    readonly onDidScroll: Event<ChatScrollEvent>;
    readonly onDidAddTurn: Event<ChatTurn>;
    readonly transcript: readonly ChatTurn[];
    readonly scroll: ChatScrollState;
    revealTurn(turnId: string): Thenable<void>;
    setDecorations(decorationType: ChatDecorationType, ranges: ChatDecorationRange[]): void;
  }

  export interface ChatScrollState {
    readonly scrollTop: number;     // px from top
    readonly scrollHeight: number;  // total px
    readonly viewportHeight: number;
  }

  export interface ChatScrollEvent {
    readonly scroll: ChatScrollState;
  }

  export interface ChatTurn {
    readonly id: string;
    readonly role: 'user' | 'assistant' | 'system' | 'tool';
    readonly timestamp: number;
    readonly content: ChatContentPart[];   // markdown, tool calls, tool results, errors
    readonly status?: 'ok' | 'e

## Environment
- VS Code version: 1.132.0
- Copilot Chat extension version: 0.60.0
- Provider: Custom Endpoint (`vendor: "customendpoint"`), `apiType: "chat-completions"`
- Backend: self-hosted vLLM 0.22.1 (OpenAI-compatible `/v1/chat/completions`, streaming)
- Model tested: `moonshotai/Kimi-K2.6` (reproducible on any model behind this backend)

## Summary
When a Custom Endpoint / BYOK chat-completions model streams a delta chunk that includes an **empty** `tool_calls` array alongside real `content` (e.g. `{"content":" I","tool_calls":[]}`), the response text is silently discarded. The request completes successfully (`finish_reason:"stop"`), output tokens are correctly counted/billed, but the chat UI shows "Sorry, no response was returned" because no text ever reaches the chat view.

## Root cause
In `extensions/copilot/src/platform/networking/node/stream.ts`, `SSEProcessor.processSSEInner()`:

```ts
let handled = true;
if (choice.delta?.tool_calls) {
    // ... StreamingToolCalls.update(choice) — iterates the array, no-op if empty
} else if (/* other cases */) {
    ...
} else {
    handled = false;
}

if (!handled) {
    solution.append(choice); // <-- this reads choice.delta.content
    if (await emitSolution()) { continue; }
}
```

`if (choice.delta?.tool_calls)` only checks **truthiness**. An empty array `[]` is truthy in JavaScript, so this branch is entered even when there are zero actual tool calls. Because the branch is entered, `handled` stays `true` (it's never reset to `false`), so the final `if (!handled) { solution.append(choice); ... }` — the only code path that reads `choice.delta.content` — is skipped. The real text in that delta is dropped.

This repeats on every content-bearing chunk in the stream if the backend attaches `tool_calls: []` to all of them (confirmed via raw curl capture — see below), so 100% of the model's answer text is lost, chunk by chunk, while the model still finishes normally and tokens are still billed.

## Suggested fix
Check for a non-empty array instead of mere presence:

```ts
if (choice.delta?.tool_calls?.length) {
```

## Reproduction
Raw SSE captured directly from the backend for a trivial prompt ("what model are you"), replayed with the exact request VS Code sent (temperature, tools, messages all identical):

```
data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"finish_reason":null}], ...}
data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning":" The"},"finish_reason":null}], ...}
... (reasoning deltas omitted) ...
data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":" I","tool_calls":[]},"finish_reason":null}], ...}
data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":" am","tool_calls":[]},"finish_reason":null}], ...}
data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":" using","tool_calls":[]},"finish_reason":null}], ...}
...
data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"**.","tool_calls":[]},"finish_reason":null}], ...}
data: {"choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{},"finish_reason":"stop"}], ...}
data: [DONE]
```

- `system_fingerprint: vllm-0.22.1-tp8-327f7987`
- The reasoning field is delivered as `delta.reasoning` (not `reasoning_content`), which is a separate (correctly handled) quirk.
- Sending the identical request directly via `curl`/Node `https` against the backend reproduces this SSE shape 100% of the time (6/6 test runs).
- A local proxy that strips the empty `tool_calls` key from delta chunks before they reach VS Code fully resolves the issue, confirming the root cause above.

Reopening a remote session can reuse a stale cached chat model that will never get updated if it was disconnected when the chat completed. The chat appears in progress in the chat, but looks idle in the session list.

```typescript
const existingRef = this.acquireExistingSession(sessionResource, debugOwner);
if (existingRef) {
	this.trace('loadRemoteSession', `reused existing model for ${sessionResource.toString()}`);
	return existingRef;
}
```
This path returns the existing `ChatModel` before resolving the reconnected provider's session with `getOrCreateChatSession`. If the cached model was attached to the old connection when it disconnected, it never receives the authoritative completed snapshot: its completion observable remains in progress and response parts produced while disconnected are never added to the conversation.

## Summary

While investigating a renderer CPU spike reported when opening a very long Copilot session ("Session database redesing"), the root cause traced to the pending-confirmation/approval detection path: on every non-quiet response change, two independent full linear scans of the response's part array run — one inside `ChatResponseModel.isPendingConfirmation` (`chatModel.ts`), and a second, redundant one inside `AgentSessionApprovalModel._trackModel`'s autorun (`agentSessionApprovalModel.ts`).

The specific O(N²) blowup that made this visible — replaying every historical response part as its own non-quiet notification during session restore — has already been fixed separately (batched restore now fires one notification instead of N). This issue is about what's left: **the scan itself is still O(N) per notification**, which matters for:
- The single "final" notification after a long restore (still one full pass over every part).
- Live streaming of a long response, where most `updateContent` calls are intentionally non-quiet (the user needs to see them), so a response with many streamed parts pays an O(N) rescan on every token/part added — effectively O(N²) over the life of one long-running turn, independent of restore.

## Perf evidence

From the original trace: the dominant 2.19s long task's stack bottomed out in `readObservable` (~4463 samples / 581.7 ms self time) under an anonymous observable computation that maps to `agentSessionApprovalModel.ts` (`_trackModel`'s `autorun`). `UpdateLayoutTree` (435.5 ms total, max 4.3 ms) and `Paint` (86.5 ms total, max 1.6 ms) were negligible by comparison — this is synchronous JS/observable work, not rendering.

## Analysis

`ChatResponseModel.isPendingConfirmation` (see [chatModel.ts](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/main/src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/common/model/chatModel.ts), the `_pendingInfo` derived value in the constructor):

```ts
const _pendingInfo = signal.map((_value, r): string | undefined => {
    signal.read(r);
    for (const part of this._response.value) {
        if (part.kind === 'toolInvocation') { ... }
        if (part.kind === 'confirmation' && !part.isUsed) { ... }
        if (part.kind === 'questionCarousel' && !part.isUsed) { ... }
        if (part.kind === 'planReview' && !part.isUsed) { ... }
        if (part.kind === 'elicitation2' && part.state.read(r) === ElicitationState.Pending) { ... }
    }
    return undefined;
});
```

`signal` is an `observableSignalFromEvent` over `this.onDidChange`, which fires on every non-quiet `updateContent`/`_contentChanged` call. So this full-array scan re-runs on every streamed token/part for as long as the response is open, not just at restore.

`AgentSessionApprovalModel._trackModel` (see [agentSessionApprovalModel.ts](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/main/src/vs/workbench/contrib/chat/browser/agentSessions/agentSessionApprovalModel.ts)) then does its own **second** full scan immediately after reading `isPendingConfirmation`:

```ts
